I would also add three architectural safeguards:

Stable IDs. Psychology techniques get P identifiers, stories get S, visual elements get V, and Kait moves retain K01–K53. This makes your existing material interoperable.

A compatibility registry. Some loops combine naturally, others duplicate one another, and some compete for the same paragraph position. Store those relationships rather than hardcoding a single preferred stack.

Outcome measurement. Track not only whether a technique was used, but whether it survived editing, improved author-rated clarity, or performed better in actual reader comprehension tests. Usage frequency alone cannot establish effectiveness.

That last part is what allows your system to evolve from a writing assistant into a genuine editorial learning system.
§7. The additional feature I'd build: the Argument Coverage Map
This is the part that could make your 15-point workflow especially useful.

Instead of only highlighting which rhetorical moves have been used, the system highlights which argumentative obligations have been satisfied.
Argument 07 — Coverage

Illustrative state

Claim clearly defined

Supporting evidence

Reasoning connects evidence to claim
Review

Strongest objection addressed

Boundary stated


Coverage is a structural check, not proof that the argument is sound. Evidence and reasoning still require validation.

This means your writing environment could tell you:

You've already used a mirror and a Socratic question. You don't need another rhetorical device here. What this argument lacks is a fair treatment of the strongest objection.

That's much more valuable than simply recommending another impressive-sounding paragraph.

The one structural decision I would make now
I would keep your existing Loops Method and expand it into four interconnected libraries:
1. Argument Library — the jobs each block must accomplish, including claim, evidence, inference, objection, and boundary.
2. Technique Library — your persuasive psychology principles, Kait's 53 moves, and the new story loops.
3. Presentation Library — paragraph rhythm, tables, quotations, diagrams, MTL units, callouts, and progressive disclosure.
4. Learning Library — detection results, author choices, combinations, revision history, and eventual reader outcomes.
The author controls the argument. The libraries supply possibilities. The API helps with individual tasks. The document continuously records what actually happened.

And the crucial insight is this:

You don't want 15 paragraphs written 15 different ways merely for variety. You want 15 arguments, each equipped with the most suitable combination of reasoning, storytelling, psychology, and presentation techniques to make that particular point understandable and defensible.

I think that's the system you've been reaching for. And it gives us a much clearer foundation for structuring the actual loops.json, argument-block schema, right-rail interface, and targeted API calls without replacing the method you've already built.